Ladybird now resolves the Auto motion preference from the system. On
macOS, it reads NSWorkspace’s accessibilityDisplayShouldReduceMotion,
and follows it through the notification that Blink, Gecko and WebKit
observe for the same setting (BrowserAccessibilityStateImplMac,
MOZLookAndFeelDynamicChangeObserver, WKAccessibilitySettingsObserver):
NSWorkspaceAccessibilityDisplayOptionsDidChangeNotification. On Linux,
it reads the settings portal’s reduced-motion, which KDE’s portal
derives from the AnimationDurationFactor of kdeglobals, and on older
portals, GNOME’s enable-animations or that factor itself.

On Linux, Gecko and Blink read GTK’s gtk-enable-animations instead
(nsLookAndFeel::InitializeGlobalSettings(), GtkUi::AnimationsEnabled()),
and Blink’s Qt path reads the widget animation duration that Qt
reports (QtShim::GetAnimationDurationMs()). Ladybird has no GTK, so it
reads the desktop settings the portal reports. All three put an explicit
choice in the browser first, as Ladybird’s Reduce and No Preference menu
items do. Read from source, not run: Chromium e6c8d7c15aeb2, Firefox
41db707342fe, WebKit c3a7f3c0cfe0.
